 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Fabrics are made from fibres, which can be natural or man-made.\nNatural fibres:\n• cotton (from the cotton plant): cool, absorbs sweat, easy to wash, creases; used for pagnes, shirts, uniforms;\n• linen (from the flax plant): strong, cool, creases a lot;\n• silk (from silkworm cocoons): smooth, shiny, soft, expensive; needs gentle washing;\n• wool (from sheep): warm, can shrink in hot water.\nMan-made (synthetic) fibres: polyester, nylon, acrylic: strong, do not crease much, dry quickly, but are hot to wear and melt with a hot iron.\nBlends (polyester-cotton) combine the advantages of both.\nFor embroidery, cotton and linen with an even weave are easiest to work on.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {},
  "problem": "Why are cotton clothes good for the hot season in Cameroon?",
  "steps": [
   "Cotton is a natural fibre that lets air pass.",
   "It absorbs sweat, so the body stays cool and dry."
  ],
  "answer": "Cotton is cool and absorbs sweat.",
  "checks": []
 },
